Adds apps/controller-ui/src/app/platform/native.ts â€” the single door between the
UI and Android. Nothing else may touch window.AndroidBridge from now on.

The bridge is 46 methods reached as (window as any).AndroidBridge?.foo?.(), so a
wrong name is invisible: the optional chain swallows it and the caller quietly
takes its fallback. That is how the removed patch called getVersionName() â€” the
real method is getAppVersionName â€” and would have shown a fake "1.0.0" forever.
Typed, that is a compile error. Every wrapper is total: no bridge, missing
method, or a throw from Kotlin returns a declared default instead of blanking
the UI, so `npm run dev` in a desktop browser still works.

getAppInfo() returns version/build/channel from BuildConfig, and getDeviceInfo()
/getDeviceLabel() return the real phone. Unavailable values stay empty rather
than defaulting, so callers hide the row instead of inventing content.

Installation identity (getInstallId): a random uuid for this install on this
phone, created on first call. Not an account and not a hardware id â€” guests get
one, so device management and layout ownership need no sign-in, and it is the
tie-breaker when one account edits a layout on two phones. It carries no
tracking identity and dies with the app.

Backed by two new bridge methods, prefGet/prefSet over the existing "gamepados"
SharedPreferences (the file the storage migration already uses), with a
localStorage fallback under the same key so an install upgrading into a
bridge-capable build keeps its id. Native storage matters here because
localStorage is cleared with WebView data â€” a cache wipe would otherwise turn
one phone into a second device. These carry identifiers, not secrets; auth
tokens will need EncryptedSharedPreferences, a separate change.

Also settles an open question: the WebView serves from
https://appassets.androidplatform.net, not file://.

Verified: gradle assembleDirectDebug exits 0, app-direct-debug.apk (6.74 MB)
produced, bridge count 44 -> 46. prefGet/prefSet are not yet exercised at
runtime â€” that happens when the Account screen reads them in Phase 3.
